text:
maria: Quick ping—planning a Terraform change to let payments pods decrypt KMS-wrapped secrets. Using IRSA (serviceAccount payments/payments-sa). Want a quick review before I open the PR.

kevin: drop the TF snippet when you have it, happy to eyeball.

maria: ```hcl
data "aws_iam_openid_connect_provider" "eks" {
  arn = "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:oidc-provider/oidc.eks.region.amazonaws.com/id/EXAMPLED"
}

data "aws_iam_policy_document" "irsa_trust" {
  statement {
    effect = "Allow"
    principals {
      type        = "Federated"
      identifiers = [data.aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.eks.arn]
    }
    actions = ["sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity"]
    condition {
      test     = "StringEquals"
      variable = "oidc.eks.region.amazonaws.com/id/EXAMPLED:sub"
      values   = ["system:serviceaccount:payments:payments-sa"]
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "payments_irsa" {
  name               = "payments-irsa-role"
  assume_role_policy = data.aws_iam_policy_document.irsa_trust.json
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "payments_kms" {
  role = aws_iam_role.payments_irsa.id
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [{
      Action = [
        "kms:Decrypt",
        "kms:GenerateDataKey"
      ]
      Effect   = "Allow"
      Resource = aws_kms_key.app_key.arn
    }]
  })
}
```

priyanka: Looks fine at a glance. A couple of notes: 1) You need to ensure the KMS key policy allows that role principal OR you must create a KMS grant. 2) If the key is cross-account we always use an explicit aws_kms_grant + condition on the grantee principal. Which are you planning?

maria: Key is in same account but our current key policy only allows root + explicit grants. I was leaning to creating a grant via TF to avoid touching key policy.

josh: Heads up — grants are fine but they live on the key and can be forgotten. aws_kms_grant in TF will create a grant that doesn't auto-clean when the role is deleted unless you explicitly retire it. If you care about lifecycle, adding the role to the key policy is cleaner.

kevin: Example key policy snippet?

priyanka: ```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowPaymentsIRSA",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/payments-irsa-role" },
      "Action": ["kms:Decrypt", "kms:GenerateDataKey"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringLike": { "aws:PrincipalArn": "arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/payments-irsa-role/*" }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

maria: Nice. One more question: will the pod use kms:Decrypt directly or only kms:GenerateDataKey? We're envelope-decrypting secrets in the app using the local KMS client.

josh: If you do local envelope decryption you need GenerateDataKey (to get plaintext data key) and sometimes Decrypt depending on how you implemented the envelope flow. SecretsManager/SSM might call Decrypt under the hood. I recommend granting both Decrypt and GenerateDataKey.

infra-bot: Please remember to add a changelog entry and tag the PR with `kms-change` for review routing.

maria: Will open PR in ~30m. @kevin @priyanka can you do a quick review once it's up? Any objections to preferring key policy over grants? I lean key policy for lifecycle reasons.

kevin: Key policy is cleaner long-term. If you do that, make sure CI or our deploy runbook has permission to update key policies (or we route through a control-plane admin). :+1:

priyanka: Agree. If you must use grants (eg. temporary rollout or emergency patch) add automation to retire them. Otherwise go key policy.

maria: Thanks folks — PR will include the TF plan output and proposed key policy change. I'll post the plan snippet here when ready.
